{"name":"Oat Milk","hex":"#dedacd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oat milk","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A milky liquid made from oats and used as a milk substitute, cooking ingredient or beverage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oat_milk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oat milk","blocks":[{"text":"Oat milk is a plant milk derived from whole oat grains by extracting the plant material with water. Oat milk has a creamy texture and mild oatmeal-like flavor, and is manufactured in various flavors, such as sweetened, unsweetened, vanilla, and chocolate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Oat_milk_glass_and_bottles.jpg/330px-Oat_milk_glass_and_bottles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oat_milk","wikidata":"Q20972637"},{"title":"Oat","blocks":[{"text":"The oat, sometimes called the common oat, is a species of cereal grass (Avena) grown for fodder and for its seed, which is known by the same name. Oats appear to have been domesticated as a secondary crop, as their seeds resembled those of other cereals closely enough for them to be included by early cultivators. Oats tolerate cold winters less well than cereals such as wheat, barley, and rye, but need less summer heat and more rain, making them important in areas such as Northwest Europe that have cool, wet summers. They can tolerate low-nutrient and acid soils. Oats grow thickly and vigorously, allowing them to outcompete many weeds, and compared to other cereals are relatively free from diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/AvenaSativa3.jpg/330px-AvenaSativa3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oat","wikidata":"Q12104"},{"title":"Milk","blocks":[{"text":"Milk is usually a white liquid food produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of nutrition for young mammals before they are able to digest solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including calcium and protein, as well as lactose and saturated fat; the enzyme lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity. The first milk, which is called colostrum, contains antibodies and immune-modulating components that strengthen the immune system against many diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk","wikidata":"Q8495"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Avena-sativa.jpg/250px-Avena-sativa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Avena-sativa.jpg/500px-Avena-sativa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Avena-sativa.jpg/250px-Avena-sativa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAvena-sativa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg/250px-Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg/500px-Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg/250px-Avena_sativa_MHNT.BOT.2015.2.33.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_cont